---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Double or Nothing — Daf Yomi Game: Bekhorot 5"
  description="Bekhorot 5: a Roman officer named Kontrokos accuses Moses of cooking the books — a Levite headcount that's short 300 men, a treasury missing half its silver. Audit the census yourself, catch the Sanctuary's secret double-weight coin, then sort mismatched newborn litters and unravel a rabbi's wordplay on Rephidim."
>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Frank+Ruhl+Libre:wght@400;500;700;900&display=swap"
  />

  <main class="min-h-screen bg-[#14161c] !text-[#e8e3d3]">
    <!-- ============ Hero ============ -->
    <div class="border-b border-[#2a2f3b] bg-gradient-to-b from-[#1b1e26]/80 to-transparent px-4 pt-10 pb-8 text-center">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] !text-[#c9a054]">
        <a href="/daf" class="hover:!text-[#e0ba6a]">daf yomi arcade</a> · bekhorot 5 · 2026-09-23
      </p>
      <h1 class="frank text-4xl font-black tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">🪙 Double or Nothing</h1>
      <p class="frank mt-1 text-xl !text-[#e0ba6a]" dir="rtl" lang="he">מֹשֶׁה רַבְּכֶם גַּנָּב הָיָה, אוֹ קוּבְיוּסְטוּס הָיָה?</p>
      <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-xl text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]">
        A Roman officer named Kontrokos walks into the beit midrash with two accusations: the Levite
        headcount is short three hundred men, and half the Tabernacle's silver never made it into the
        building. "Was your teacher Moses a thief," he asks Rabban Yoḥanan ben Zakkai, "or a gambler
        [<em>kuveyustus</em>], or just bad at math?" <strong class="!text-[#e8e3d3]">Audit the books
        yourself</strong> on
        <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Bekhorot.5a.8" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="!text-[#e0ba6a] underline decoration-[#4a4530] hover:!text-[#f0cf85]">5a</a>,
        then sort a litter of mismatched newborns and catch two rabbis fighting over whether a place name
        is ever just a place name.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 pb-16 pt-8">
      <!-- ============ Field notes: how the daf opens ============ -->
      <details class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-[#2a2f3b] bg-[#1b1e26] p-5">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c9a054]">
          field notes — the daf opens mid-argument (5a:1–7) ▾
        </summary>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]">
          <p>
            The page opens still inside yesterday's question: once the Levites took over the firstborns'
            role, did the firstborns' own sanctity end right there in the wilderness — or only once Israel
            crossed into the land? Reish Lakish has a plain verse tied to entering the land (Exodus
            13:11–12). Rabbi Yoḥanan's side is harder to source, until Rabbi Elazar offers an unusual
            credential: <span class="frank !text-[#cfc9b0]" dir="rtl" lang="he">״רַבִּי יוֹחָנָן חֲזַאי בְּחֶילְמָא דְּמִילְּתָא מְעַלַּיְתָא אָמֵינָא״</span>
            — <em>"I saw Rabbi Yoḥanan in a dream, which is how I know I'm explaining him correctly"</em>
            — and reads the extra words "Mine they shall be" (Numbers 3:13) as the real proof.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then a twist: Rav Mordekhai tells Rav Ashi he learned the whole dispute backwards — Rabbi
            Yoḥanan and Reish Lakish's positions reversed from how Rav Ashi had it. Rav Ashi points out
            that this would also flip Rabbi Elazar's dream story onto the wrong sage. Rav Mordekhai's fix
            changes no halakha at all — only what "not sanctified" means. Rav Ashi's verdict:
            <span class="frank !text-[#cfc9b0]" dir="rtl" lang="he">״חַיָּיב אָדָם לוֹמַר בִּלְשׁוֹן רַבּוֹ״</span>
            — <em>a person must always report a teaching in his own teacher's exact words</em>, even when it
            changes nothing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- ============ ROUND 1: Kontrokos audits the books ============ -->
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-[#3a3320] bg-[#1b1e26] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c9a054]">round one · play it yourself</p>
        <h2 class="frank mt-1 text-2xl font-bold !text-[#e8e3d3]">Kontrokos audits the books</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]">
          Kontrokos the officer studied the Torah's own numbers and found two ledgers that don't balance.
          Work out where Rabban Yoḥanan ben Zakkai's defense goes, before you see it.
        </p>

        <!-- Puzzle A: the missing 300 Levites -->
        <div id="puzzle-a" class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-[#3a3320] bg-[#14161c] p-4">
          <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e0ba6a]">Ledger 1 — the Levite headcount</p>
          <div class="mt-3 space-y-1 text-sm !text-[#c9c4ac]">
            <div class="flex justify-between"><span dir="rtl" lang="he" class="frank">גֵּרְשׁוֹן</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#e8e3d3]">7,500</span></div>
            <div class="flex justify-between"><span dir="rtl" lang="he" class="frank">קְהָת</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#e8e3d3]">8,600</span></div>
            <div class="flex justify-between"><span dir="rtl" lang="he" class="frank">מְרָרִי</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#e8e3d3]">6,200</span></div>
            <div class="mt-1 flex justify-between border-t border-[#2a2f3b] pt-1 font-bold">
              <span>Sum of families (Numbers 3:22,28,34)</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#e0ba6a]">22,300</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex justify-between">
              <span>Torah's stated total (Numbers 3:39)</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#e8e3d3]">22,000</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex justify-between font-bold">
              <span class="!text-[#b25b3c]">Discrepancy</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#b25b3c]">−300</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm !text-[#c9c4ac]">Kontrokos: "Where did the three hundred go?"</p>
          <div id="puzzle-a-choices" class="mt-2 grid gap-2">
            <button type="button" class="choice-btn" data-correct="false">A scribal error crept into the census</button>
            <button type="button" class="choice-btn" data-correct="true">Those 300 were firstborn Levites themselves</button>
            <button type="button" class="choice-btn" data-correct="false">They died before the count was finalized</button>
          </div>
          <div class="reveal mt-3 hidden rounded-lg border border-[#3a3320] bg-[#1b1e26] p-3 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9c4ac]"></div>
        </div>

        <!-- Puzzle B: the missing silver -->
        <div id="puzzle-b" class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-[#3a3320] bg-[#14161c] p-4">
          <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#e0ba6a]">Ledger 2 — the Tabernacle silver</p>
          <div class="mt-3 space-y-1 text-sm !text-[#c9c4ac]">
            <div class="flex justify-between"><span>603,550 men × ½ shekel (Exodus 38:26)</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#e8e3d3]">301,775 shekels</span></div>
            <div class="flex justify-between">
              <span>Collected, in talents (1 talent = 1,500 shekels)</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#e0ba6a]">201 talents, 11 maneh</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex justify-between">
              <span>Spent on sockets (Exodus 38:27)</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#e8e3d3]">100 talents</span>
            </div>
            <div class="flex justify-between font-bold">
              <span class="!text-[#b25b3c]">Discrepancy</span><span class="num font-mono !text-[#b25b3c]">≈ half the treasury</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm !text-[#c9c4ac]">
            Kontrokos: "Was Moses your teacher a thief, or a gambler, or just not expert in accounting?"
          </p>
          <div id="puzzle-b-choices" class="mt-2 grid gap-2">
            <button type="button" class="choice-btn" data-correct="false">Moses kept the difference for himself</button>
            <button type="button" class="choice-btn" data-correct="true">The Sanctuary's own maneh weighed double a standard one</button>
            <button type="button" class="choice-btn" data-correct="false">The extra silver was lost crossing the desert</button>
          </div>
          <div class="reveal mt-3 hidden rounded-lg border border-[#3a3320] bg-[#1b1e26] p-3 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9c4ac]"></div>
        </div>

        <!-- Bonus: one-sixth from the outside -->
        <div id="puzzle-c" class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-[#233221] bg-[#14161c] p-4">
          <p class="text-xs font-bold !text-[#5f9c88]">Bonus ledger — Ezekiel's maneh (5b:1–2)</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#c9c4ac]">
            Rav Ḥisda's proof for the doubled maneh comes from an unrelated verse: <span class="frank !text-[#cfc9b0]" dir="rtl" lang="he">״עֶשְׂרִים שְׁקָלִים חֲמִשָּׁה וְעֶשְׂרִים שְׁקָלִים עֲשָׂרָה וַחֲמִשָּׁה שֶׁקֶל הַמָּנֶה יִהְיֶה לָכֶם״</span>
            (Ezekiel 45:12) — 20 + 25 + 10 + 5 shekels make one maneh. That's sixty shekels, not the
            Sanctuary's fifty. A community may pad a measure, but never past one-sixth — and the Gemara
            proves that "one-sixth" is counted from the <em>final</em> total, not the original. Find the padding
            that makes Ezekiel's numbers work.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-3 flex items-baseline justify-between text-xs">
            <label for="pad-slider" class="!text-[#98a0ad]">Padding, in shekels, added to the base 50</label>
            <span id="pad-val" class="num font-mono !text-[#5f9c88]"></span>
          </div>
          <input id="pad-slider" type="range" min="0" max="20" value="0" step="1" class="mt-2 w-full" />
          <div id="pad-readout" class="mt-3 rounded-lg border border-[#233221] bg-[#1b1e26] p-3 text-sm !text-[#c9c4ac]"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ ROUND 2: sort the litter ============ -->
      <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-[#2a2f3b] bg-[#1b1e26] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c9a054]">round two · the mishna, בכורות ה׳ ב</p>
        <h2 class="frank mt-1 text-2xl font-bold !text-[#e8e3d3]">Sort the mismatched litter</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]">
          <span class="frank !text-[#cfc9b0]" dir="rtl" lang="he">״פֶּטֶר חֲמוֹר״ ״פֶּטֶר חֲמוֹר״</span> — the Torah
          writes "firstborn donkey" twice, so a newborn only has donkey-firstborn status if <em>both</em>
          the mother and the newborn are donkeys. But eating status runs on a totally different rule:
          it only ever follows the mother. Guess each verdict before you check it.
        </p>
        <div id="litter-cards" class="mt-4 space-y-4"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ ROUND 3: name or derash ============ -->
      <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-[#2a2f3b] bg-[#1b1e26] p-5">
        <p class="text-[11px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] !text-[#c9a054]">round three · a name, or a nickname? (5b:3–6)</p>
        <h2 class="frank mt-1 text-2xl font-bold !text-[#e8e3d3]">Rabbi Chanina's questions</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]">
          Rabbi Chanina once cornered Rabbi Eliezer in the great study hall with a string of questions.
          First, why donkeys — and not horses or camels — get redeemed at all. Then, two places on Israel's
          route that might not be simple place names. Rabbi Eliezer always answers the same way: it's just
          the name. A baraita preserves what Rabbi Yehoshua thought instead. Flip each card.
        </p>
        <div id="dialogue-cards" class="mt-4 space-y-4"></div>
      </div>

      <!-- ============ Learn the real daf ============ -->
      <div class="mt-14 border-t border-[#2a2f3b] pt-8">
        <h3 class="frank mb-3 text-xl font-bold !text-[#c9a054]">Learn the real daf</h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]">
          Bekhorot 5 closes the wilderness-sanctity sugya with Kontrokos's audit, then opens a new mishna:
          a cow that gives birth to something donkey-shaped, or a donkey to something horse-shaped, owes
          nothing as a firstborn — the Torah's double "firstborn donkey" demands the newborn match its
          mother's species exactly, with one carve-out for partial resemblance. Rav Yehuda and Rabbi Yosei
          HaGelili trace that same principle to two different verses, and argue over which kind of sanctity
          — inherent, or measured in value — is the model for the other.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-xs">
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Bekhorot.5a" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="rounded-full border border-[#3a3320] bg-[#1b1e26]/60 px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#c9a054] hover:border-[#c9a054]">Bekhorot 5 on Sefaria ↗</a>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-[#2a2f3b] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#98a0ad]">Steinsaltz ×49</span>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-[#2a2f3b] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#98a0ad]">Rashi ×44</span>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-[#2a2f3b] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#98a0ad]">Rabbeinu Gershom ×42</span>
          <span class="rounded-full border border-[#2a2f3b] px-3 py-1.5 !text-[#98a0ad]">Toldot Yaakov Yosef ×15</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 text-xs !text-[#5c6270]">
          Part of the <a href="/daf" class="underline decoration-[#3a3f4d] hover:!text-[#98a0ad]">Daf Yomi Arcade</a> —
          a new game from every daf. Texts and links via the Sefaria API.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <style is:global>
    .frank {
      font-family: 'Frank Ruhl Libre', Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
    }
    .num {
      font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    }

    .choice-btn {
      text-align: left;
      border-radius: 0.7rem;
      border: 1px solid #3a3f4d;
      background: #14161c;
      color: #c9c4ac;
      padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
      font-size: 0.875rem;
      transition: background-color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s, color 0.15s;
    }
    .choice-btn:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: #c9a054; color: #e8e3d3; }
    .choice-btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #c9a054; outline-offset: 2px; }
    .choice-btn:disabled { cursor: default; }
    .choice-btn.choice-correct { border-color: #5f9c88; background: #16241f; color: #bfe8d8 !important; }
    .choice-btn.choice-wrong { border-color: #b25b3c; background: #241713; color: #f0c3b0 !important; }

    .verdict-btn {
      border-radius: 999px;
      border: 1px solid #3a3f4d;
      background: #14161c;
      color: #98a0ad;
      padding: 0.35rem 0.8rem;
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      transition: background-color 0.15s, border-color 0.15s, color 0.15s;
    }
    .verdict-btn:hover:not(:disabled) { border-color: #c9a054; color: #e8e3d3; }
    .verdict-btn:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid #c9a054; outline-offset: 2px; }
    .verdict-btn[aria-pressed='true'] { background: #c9a054; border-color: #c9a054; color: #14161c !important; }
    .verdict-btn.verdict-correct { background: #5f9c88; border-color: #5f9c88; color: #0e1613 !important; }
    .verdict-btn.verdict-wrong { background: #b25b3c; border-color: #b25b3c; color: #1c0f0b !important; }

    .toggle-track {
      display: inline-flex;
      border-radius: 999px;
      border: 1px solid #3a3f4d;
      background: #14161c;
      padding: 0.2rem;
    }
    .toggle-btn {
      border-radius: 999px;
      padding: 0.3rem 0.75rem;
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      color: #98a0ad;
      transition: background-color 0.15s, color 0.15s;
    }
    .toggle-btn[aria-pressed='true'] { background: #c9a054; color: #14161c !important; }

    input[type='range'] {
      -webkit-appearance: none;
      appearance: none;
      height: 6px;
      border-radius: 999px;
      background: linear-gradient(90deg, #5f9c88, #c9a054 60%, #b25b3c);
      outline: none;
    }
    input[type='range']::-webkit-slider-thumb {
      -webkit-appearance: none;
      appearance: none;
      width: 18px;
      height: 18px;
      border-radius: 50%;
      background: #e8e3d3;
      border: 2px solid #14161c;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
    input[type='range']::-moz-range-thumb {
      width: 18px;
      height: 18px;
      border-radius: 50%;
      background: #e8e3d3;
      border: 2px solid #14161c;
      cursor: pointer;
    }

    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .choice-btn, .verdict-btn, .toggle-btn, #grid > div { transition: none; }
    }
  </style>

  <script>
    const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;

    // ---------- Generic multiple-choice reveal ----------
    function mcCard(rootId: string, revealHtml: string) {
      const root = $(rootId);
      const btns = Array.from(root.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.choice-btn'));
      let answered = false;
      btns.forEach((btn) => {
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (answered) return;
          answered = true;
          const correct = btn.dataset.correct === 'true';
          btns.forEach((b) => {
            b.disabled = true;
            if (b.dataset.correct === 'true') b.classList.add('choice-correct');
            else if (b === btn && !correct) b.classList.add('choice-wrong');
          });
          const reveal = root.querySelector('.reveal') as HTMLElement;
          reveal.innerHTML = revealHtml;
          reveal.classList.remove('hidden');
        });
      });
    }

    mcCard('puzzle-a', `
      <p class="font-bold !text-[#5f9c88]">Right — Abaye explains why (Bekhorot 5a:9)</p>
      <p class="mt-1" dir="rtl" lang="he" class="frank">דַּיּוֹ לַבְּכוֹר שֶׁיַּפְקִיעַ קְדוּשַּׁת עַצְמוֹ</p>
      <p class="mt-1">"It is enough for a firstborn to abrogate his own sanctity." Those 300 Levites were
      themselves firstborns — their hands were full clearing their <em>own</em> firstborn status. A
      firstborn Levite can't also stand in to redeem an Israelite firstborn. Drop them from the redeeming
      pool, and 22,000 Levites are left to cover the Israelite firstborns.</p>
      <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Bekhorot.5a.9" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-2 inline-block !text-[#5f9c88] hover:!text-[#8fd0b8] hover:underline">Bekhorot 5a:9 ↗</a>
    `);

    mcCard('puzzle-b', `
      <p class="font-bold !text-[#5f9c88]">Right — Rabban Yoḥanan ben Zakkai's answer (5a:12)</p>
      <p class="mt-1" dir="rtl" lang="he" class="frank">מֹשֶׁה רַבֵּינוּ גִּזְבָּר נֶאֱמָן הָיָה, וּמָנֶה שֶׁל קוֹדֶשׁ כָּפוּל הָיָה</p>
      <p class="mt-1">"Moses was a trustworthy treasurer — the Sanctuary's own maneh weighed double a
      standard one: fifty shekels, not twenty-five." A hundred sockets-talents, reckoned at Sanctuary
      weight, come out to two hundred talents at standard weight — bringing Moses's number back in line
      with what was collected. The Gemara chases the proof through two rejected readings (the hook-silver
      and the copper-offering verses) before Rav Ḥisda lands it for good, from an unrelated verse in
      Ezekiel — see the bonus ledger below.</p>
      <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/Bekhorot.5a.12" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-2 inline-block !text-[#5f9c88] hover:!text-[#8fd0b8] hover:underline">Bekhorot 5a:12 ↗</a>
    `);

    // ---------- Bonus: one-sixth from the outside ----------
    const BASE = 50;
    const padSlider = $('pad-slider') as HTMLInputElement;
    const padVal = $('pad-val');
    const padReadout = $('pad-readout');
    function updatePad() {
      const pad = Number(padSlider.value);
      const final = BASE + pad;
      const frac = pad / final;
      padVal.textContent = `${pad}`;
      const isSixth = pad === 10;
      padReadout.className = `mt-3 rounded-lg border p-3 text-sm ${isSixth ? 'border-[#5f9c88] bg-[#16241f] !text-[#bfe8d8]' : 'border-[#233221] bg-[#1b1e26] !text-[#c9c4ac]'}`;
      padReadout.innerHTML = `
        <span class="num font-mono">${BASE} + ${pad} = ${final}</span> shekels ·
        padding is <span class="num font-mono">${pad}/${final}</span> of the final total
        ${isSixth
          ? ' — exactly one-sixth. This is Ezekiel\'s own maneh: 20 + 25 + 10 + 5 = 60 shekels. The extra ten is the community\'s maximum allowed padding, counted from the *final* sixty — not the original fifty.'
          : (frac > 1 / 6 ? ' — too much. A community may never pad a measure by more than one-sixth.' : ' — keep sliding.')}
      `;
    }
    padSlider.addEventListener('input', updatePad);
    updatePad();

    // ---------- Round 2: the litter sorter ----------
    type LitterCase = {
      mother: string;
      offspring: string;
      resemblance: string;
      firstborn: 'exempt' | 'obligated' | null;
      eating: 'permitted' | 'forbidden' | null;
      note: string;
      ref: string;
    };
    const LITTER: LitterCase[] = [
      {
        mother: '🐄 Cow (kosher)',
        offspring: 'a donkey-shaped calf',
        resemblance: 'no resemblance to the mother',
        firstborn: 'exempt',
        eating: 'permitted',
        note: '"Firstborn donkey" is written twice — donkey-status needs mother AND newborn to both be donkeys. This calf is neither, and it never looked like anything but the fully kosher mother it came from, so it\'s food.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:7',
      },
      {
        mother: '🫏 Donkey (non-kosher)',
        offspring: 'a horse-shaped foal',
        resemblance: 'no resemblance to the mother',
        firstborn: 'exempt',
        eating: null,
        note: 'Same doubled verse, same result: no donkey-redemption applies when the newborn doesn\'t look like a donkey.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:7',
      },
      {
        mother: '🐑 Ewe (kosher)',
        offspring: 'a goat-shaped lamb',
        resemblance: 'no resemblance at all',
        firstborn: 'exempt',
        eating: 'permitted',
        note: 'The parallel mishna (16b) applies the same rule to inherent sanctity: full firstborn status needs the newborn to match its mother completely.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:9',
      },
      {
        mother: '🐑 Ewe (kosher)',
        offspring: 'a mostly goat-shaped lamb',
        resemblance: 'one sheep siman present',
        firstborn: 'obligated',
        eating: 'permitted',
        note: '"אַךְ" — "But" — the Torah carves out an exception: even partial resemblance is enough to trigger firstborn sanctity after all.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:11',
      },
      {
        mother: '🐐 Goat',
        offspring: 'an ordinary goat kid',
        resemblance: 'full resemblance',
        firstborn: 'obligated',
        eating: 'permitted',
        note: 'The baseline case — mother and newborn match, sanctity applies exactly as expected.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:9',
      },
      {
        mother: '🐫 Camel (non-kosher)',
        offspring: 'a fully kosher-looking newborn',
        resemblance: 'looks completely kosher',
        firstborn: null,
        eating: 'forbidden',
        note: 'Eating status never asks what the newborn looks like — only what the mother was. "What emerges from the non-kosher is non-kosher," full stop, no matter how kosher the offspring appears.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:8',
      },
    ];

    function renderLitterCard(c: LitterCase, i: number) {
      const wrap = document.createElement('div');
      wrap.className = 'rounded-xl border border-[#233221] bg-[#14161c] p-4';
      const fbId = `fb-${i}`;
      const eatId = `eat-${i}`;
      wrap.innerHTML = `
        <p class="text-sm !text-[#c9c4ac]"><strong class="!text-[#e8e3d3]">${c.mother}</strong> gives birth to ${c.offspring} — ${c.resemblance}.</p>
        <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
          ${c.firstborn ? `
            <span class="text-xs !text-[#7c8394]">Firstborn status?</span>
            <div class="flex gap-1.5" id="${fbId}">
              <button type="button" class="verdict-btn" data-val="exempt">exempt</button>
              <button type="button" class="verdict-btn" data-val="obligated">obligated</button>
            </div>
          ` : ''}
          ${c.eating ? `
            <span class="ml-2 text-xs !text-[#7c8394]">Safe to eat?</span>
            <div class="flex gap-1.5" id="${eatId}">
              <button type="button" class="verdict-btn" data-val="permitted">permitted</button>
              <button type="button" class="verdict-btn" data-val="forbidden">forbidden</button>
            </div>
          ` : ''}
        </div>
        <div class="note mt-3 hidden rounded-lg border border-[#233221] bg-[#1b1e26] p-3 text-xs leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]"></div>
      `;
      const noteEl = wrap.querySelector('.note') as HTMLElement;
      let fbDone = !c.firstborn;
      let eatDone = !c.eating;
      function maybeReveal() {
        if (fbDone && eatDone) {
          noteEl.innerHTML = `${c.note} <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/${c.ref.replace('Bekhorot ', 'Bekhorot.').replace(':', '.')}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-2 inline-block !text-[#c9a054] hover:!text-[#e0ba6a] hover:underline">${c.ref} ↗</a>`;
          noteEl.classList.remove('hidden');
        }
      }
      if (c.firstborn) {
        const group = wrap.querySelector(`#${fbId}`) as HTMLElement;
        const btns = Array.from(group.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.verdict-btn'));
        btns.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (fbDone) return;
          fbDone = true;
          btns.forEach((x) => {
            x.disabled = true;
            if (x.dataset.val === c.firstborn) x.classList.add('verdict-correct');
            else if (x === b) x.classList.add('verdict-wrong');
          });
          maybeReveal();
        }));
      }
      if (c.eating) {
        const group = wrap.querySelector(`#${eatId}`) as HTMLElement;
        const btns = Array.from(group.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.verdict-btn'));
        btns.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener('click', () => {
          if (eatDone) return;
          eatDone = true;
          btns.forEach((x) => {
            x.disabled = true;
            if (x.dataset.val === c.eating) x.classList.add('verdict-correct');
            else if (x === b) x.classList.add('verdict-wrong');
          });
          maybeReveal();
        }));
      }
      return wrap;
    }
    const litterRoot = $('litter-cards');
    LITTER.forEach((c, i) => litterRoot.appendChild(renderLitterCard(c, i)));

    // ---------- Round 3: name or derash ----------
    type DialogueCard = {
      question: string;
      fixed?: string;
      fixedRef?: string;
      literal?: string;
      derash?: string;
      ref?: string;
    };
    const DIALOGUE: DialogueCard[] = [
      {
        question: 'Why are firstborn donkeys redeemed with a lamb, but not firstborn horses or camels?',
        fixed: 'It\'s a Torah decree — and besides, donkeys helped Israel at the Exodus: there wasn\'t a single Israelite who didn\'t leave Egypt with ninety splendid Nubian donkeys, loaded down with Egyptian silver and gold.',
        fixedRef: 'Bekhorot 5b:3',
      },
      {
        question: '"Then Amalek came and fought Israel at Rephidim" (Exodus 17:8) — what does the name mean?',
        literal: 'Rephidim was its name — a place, nothing more.',
        derash: 'They let their hands go slack [rippu] from Torah study, and that\'s why Amalek could attack — "hands grow feeble when fathers stop turning to their children" (Jeremiah 47:3).',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:4',
      },
      {
        question: '"And Israel dwelled in Shittim" (Numbers 25:1) — a place name, or a description?',
        literal: 'Shittim was its name.',
        derash: 'They occupied themselves with shetut — folly: the idolatry and immorality of the Ba\'al Pe\'or incident that follows in the very next verse.',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:5',
      },
      {
        question: '"And they called [vatikrena] the people to the sacrifices of their gods" (Numbers 25:2) — called how?',
        literal: 'Naked women met them on the road, and that is how they were drawn in.',
        derash: 'They all experienced keri, a seminal emission brought on by that very encounter — the verse\'s word "vatikrena" itself hints at "keri."',
        ref: 'Bekhorot 5b:6',
      },
    ];

    function renderDialogueCard(c: DialogueCard, i: number) {
      const wrap = document.createElement('div');
      wrap.className = 'rounded-xl border border-[#233221] bg-[#14161c] p-4';
      if (c.fixed) {
        wrap.innerHTML = `
          <p class="text-sm !text-[#c9c4ac]">${c.question}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider !text-[#c9a054]">Rabbi Eliezer says</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]">${c.fixed}</p>
          <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/${c.fixedRef!.replace('Bekhorot ', 'Bekhorot.').replace(':', '.')}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-2 inline-block text-xs !text-[#c9a054] hover:!text-[#e0ba6a] hover:underline">${c.fixedRef} ↗</a>
        `;
        return wrap;
      }
      const toggleId = `dlg-toggle-${i}`;
      const bodyId = `dlg-body-${i}`;
      wrap.innerHTML = `
        <p class="text-sm !text-[#c9c4ac]">${c.question}</p>
        <div class="mt-3 toggle-track" id="${toggleId}" role="group" aria-label="Choose a reading">
          <button type="button" class="toggle-btn" data-side="literal" aria-pressed="true">Rabbi Eliezer</button>
          <button type="button" class="toggle-btn" data-side="derash" aria-pressed="false">Rabbi Yehoshua</button>
        </div>
        <p id="${bodyId}" class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed !text-[#98a0ad]"></p>
        <a href="https://www.sefaria.org/${c.ref!.replace('Bekhorot ', 'Bekhorot.').replace(':', '.')}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="mt-2 inline-block text-xs !text-[#c9a054] hover:!text-[#e0ba6a] hover:underline">${c.ref} ↗</a>
      `;
      const body = wrap.querySelector(`#${bodyId}`) as HTMLElement;
      const btns = Array.from(wrap.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.toggle-btn'));
      function setSide(side: 'literal' | 'derash') {
        btns.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.side === side)));
        body.textContent = side === 'literal' ? c.literal! : c.derash!;
      }
      btns.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener('click', () => setSide(b.dataset.side as 'literal' | 'derash')));
      setSide('literal');
      return wrap;
    }
    const dialogueRoot = $('dialogue-cards');
    DIALOGUE.forEach((c, i) => dialogueRoot.appendChild(renderDialogueCard(c, i)));
  </script>
</Layout>
